This one builds the answer from the last character to the first. Each character from `s` is added to a new list, so the original list stays unchanged.

The dry run for `[a, b, c]` is simple: read `c`, then `b`, then `a`, giving `[c, b, a]`.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Solution {
    public ArrayList<Character> reverseString(ArrayList<Character> s) {
        ArrayList<Character> reversed = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = s.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            reversed.add(s.get(i));
        }

        return reversed;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the new list. This code does not change the input list.

If the input is empty, the loop is skipped and an empty `ArrayList` is returned.

The standard `reverse` function already performs the needed swaps from both ends. After it finishes, the vector contains the characters in the opposite order.

For a dry run with `[x, y, z]`, the first swap changes it to `[z, y, x]`. The middle character needs no swap.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<char> reverseString(vector<char>& s) {
        reverse(s.begin(), s.end());
        return s;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space. The input vector is reversed in place.

The empty and one-character cases work without a special condition because `reverse` has nothing to swap.

A direct in-place version. The left index starts at the front and the right index starts at the back. Swapping those characters and moving both indexes inward reverses the list without creating another one.

For example, `[a, b, c, d]` becomes `[d, b, c, a]`, then `[d, c, b, a]`.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<char> reverseString(vector<char>& s) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = static_cast<int>(s.size()) - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            swap(s[left], s[right]);
            left++;
            right--;
        }

        return s;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space. The input array is changed in place.

An empty list and a one-character list are already reversed, so the loop simply does not run for them.

