## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public ArrayList<Character> reverseString(ArrayList<Character> s) {
        //your code goes here
        return helper(s, 0, s.size()-1);

    }
    private ArrayList<Character> helper(ArrayList<Character> s, int left , int right){
        if (left >= right) return s;

        char temp = s.get(left);
        s.set(left, s.get(right));
        s.set(right, temp);

        return helper(s, left+1, right-1);
    }
}

## Intuition
Reversing a sequence is really just about swapping mirror-image pairs — the first element with the last, the second with the second-last, and so on, until the two pointers meet or cross in the middle. Once you see the problem as "swap outer pairs and shrink inward," recursion becomes a natural fit: each call handles one pair of swaps and then delegates the smaller, inner sub-problem (same task, shrunk range) to itself. The base case is simply when there's nothing left to swap — when the start pointer meets or passes the end pointer.

## Approach
1. Set up two pointers: start = 0 and end = s.size() - 1, marking the current boundaries of the unreversed portion of the list.
2. Base case: If start >= end, there are zero or one elements left in the range, so there's nothing more to swap — 3. return immediately. This correctly handles:
An empty list (size() - 1 = -1, so start > end right away)
A single-character list (start == end)
Even and odd-length lists alike, since for odd lengths the middle element naturally never gets swapped with anything (it stays in place, which is correct)
3. Swap step: Swap the characters at start and end using get/set on the ArrayList, with a temporary variable to hold one value during the swap.
4. Recurse inward: Call reverse(arr, start + 1, end - 1), shrinking the range by one from each side. This repeats the swap-and-shrink process until the base case is hit.
Since the swaps happen in-place on the same ArrayList reference, once recursion unwinds, s itself is fully reversed, and the original method simply returns it.

