        return s;
    }

    static void reverse(ArrayList<Character> arr , int start , int end){

        if(start >= end){
            return;
        }

        char temp = arr.get(start);
        arr.set(start ,arr.get(end));
        arr.set(end ,temp);



        reverse(arr , start+1 , end-1);


    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:**
O(n) — the recursive function is called once per pair of elements swapped, so it runs roughly n/2 times, each doing O(1) work (two gets, two sets). This simplifies to O(n) overall, where n is the size of the list.

**Space Complexity:**
O(n) — although no extra data structure is used, the recursion itself consumes stack space proportional to the depth of recursion, which is about n/2 calls in the worst case. This makes the auxiliary space O(n) due to the call stack (as opposed to O(1) if this were rewritten as an iterative loop).53:["$","$

